  <div class="container--narrow section prose">
    <p>
      The "H" in our pillars of HOPE stands for homicides — and for the
      families living with one that hasn't been solved. Some of those cases
      are officially cold. Others were never treated as homicides in the
      first place: deaths ruled accidental, undetermined, or "suspicious"
      that a family knows, in their bones, were something more. We call these
      <strong>hidden homicides</strong>, and they are at the center of why
      this organization exists. Our founder's mother, Leslie "Hope" Houston,
      was one of them — until seven months of independent research helped get
      her case reopened and charges filed.
    </p>

    <h2>What our cold case review includes</h2>
    <p>
      Everything we do is free, and everything we do is designed to be useful
      to the detectives and prosecutors who can actually act on it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Case file organization.</strong> We help families gather what
        they have — reports, correspondence, photos, timelines, social media —
        and organize it into a clean, chronological case file an investigator
        can absorb quickly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public records research.</strong> We prepare and track
        requests under Indiana's Access to Public Records Act (APRA) and
        federal FOIA: incident reports, coroner and autopsy records where
        available, dispatch logs, and court filings.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Timeline and discrepancy analysis.</strong> We build detailed
        timelines and flag gaps and contradictions between statements,
        records, and physical evidence — the places where a fresh look tends
        to matter most.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Advocacy for reexamination.</strong> When the research
        supports it, we help families make a professional, documented case to
        the agency — or the prosecutor — for reopening or reclassifying a
        death.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we are not</h2>
    <p>
      We are not detectives, and we do not interfere with active
      investigations. We do not name suspects publicly, speculate for
      audiences, or treat anyone's loss as content. Our role is to prepare
      the strongest possible factual record and put it in the hands of the
      people with the authority to act — families, their attorneys, law
      enforcement, and prosecutors. Agencies get a partner, not a critic:
      see <a href="/get-help/law-enforcement">how we work with law enforcement</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Who this program is for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Families of unsolved homicides in Indiana and surrounding states — however old the case.</li>
      <li>Families whose loved one's death was ruled accidental, undetermined, or suicide, and who have real reasons to question that ruling.</li>
      <li>Detectives and prosecutors who want organized research support on a cold case. Our help is free to agencies too.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to start</h2>
    <p>
      Start with our <a href="/get-help/families">families page</a> — it walks
      through what to expect — or go straight to
      <a href="/about/contact">contacting us</a> about your case. If you have
      information about someone else's case, you can
      <a href="/get-help/submit-a-tip">submit an anonymous tip</a>. There is
      no cost, no obligation, and no judgment about how long it has been.
      Cold does not mean closed.
    </p>
